\section*{Questions in Geometric Group Theory — Q 2.9}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Every finite-rank Artin Salvetti complex is claimed aspherical, allowing arbitrary Coxeter labels.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Choose an arbitrary Coxeter matrix \(M\) on a finite set \(S\), allowing infinite labels. Let \(W\) be its Coxeter group and \(X(W,S)\) the Artin Salvetti complex, with a \(|T|\)-cell for each spherical subset \(T\subseteq S\). The source claims
\[
 \widetilde{X(W,S)}\text{ contractible},\qquad
 \pi_n(X(W,S))=0\quad\text{for all }n\ge2.
\]
No connectedness condition is imposed on the Coxeter diagram.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A framed category records spherical parabolic cosets and the Artin action. Weighted harmonic heights and two-point estimates organize convex layers; cohomological tests on those layers remove the higher homotopy obstruction and establish the classifying-space property.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The canonical Salvetti complex is finite because a finite generator set has finitely many spherical subsets; the main theorem proves it is a classifying space, a specified model stronger than unspecified existence. The claimed conclusion supplies the requested case and extends it in the directions just specified.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 254, the scope includes finite-rank Salvetti contractibility, the 116-generator Artin CAT(0) counterexample, and arbitrary parabolic intersections with closure consequences. Each has its own Comparator statement. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ArtinCAT0.lean}, \texttt{ArtinParabolicIntersections.lean}, \texttt{HarmonicArtin.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.ArtinCAT0.main}, \texttt{OAI.HarmonicArtin.salvetti\_cover\_contractible}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

